---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/services.css';
import '../../styles/editorial-pages.css';
const rows = [
  ['Cloud compute',6000,8000,10000,'Separate usage growth from rate and configuration changes'],
  ['AI API usage',2000,3500,5000,'Match provider charges to accepted-result records'],
  ['Data storage and transfer',1000,1400,2000,'Check storage growth and transfer drivers'],
  ['Observability',600,800,1000,'Check retention, ingestion, and service ownership'],
  ['Team software',1200,1500,1800,'Separate added seats, plan changes, and renewal terms'],
];
const totals = [1,2,3].map(i => rows.reduce((sum,row) => sum + Number(row[i]),0));
const money = (value: number) => new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US',{style:'currency',currency:'USD',maximumFractionDigits:0}).format(value);
---
<BaseLayout title="Cost & Workflow Review Examples | Cloud & Capital" description="Explore fictional technology cost and AI workflow reviews, with labeled assumptions, calculations, and next steps. See what the work can look like.">
  <main id="main-content" class="offer-page example-page"><div class="offer-shell">
    <p class="offer-eyebrow">What the work can look like</p><h1>Three questions.<br /><em>Three useful reviews.</em></h1>
    <div class="example-note"><p><strong>Fictional examples.</strong> The businesses, records, costs, timings, and outcomes below are invented to demonstrate the proposed services. These are not client results or testimonials.</p></div>
    <div class="offer-actions"><a href="#cost">Technology costs</a><a href="#ai-bill">An AI bill</a><a href="#workflow">Recurring reporting</a><a href="/services">Back to services →</a></div>
    <section id="cost" class="example-section" aria-labelledby="cost-title"><p class="offer-eyebrow">Technology Cost Review</p><h2 id="cost-title">“Our product is growing.<br />Why is the technology bill growing faster?”</h2><p>A fictional software startup supplies three months of cloud, AI, and software bills, plus matched AI usage and accepted-result records. The illustration excludes tax, credits, prepaid annual charges, and staff costs. These are invented records, not a client engagement.</p>
      <p class="table-hint">Scroll across for all three months and the next checks.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Fictional technology costs"><table><caption>Illustrative monthly bills in USD</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Cost area</th><th scope="col">Month 1</th><th scope="col">Month 2</th><th scope="col">Month 3</th><th scope="col">Next check</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows.map(row => <tr><th scope="row">{row[0]}</th><td>{money(Number(row[1]))}</td><td>{money(Number(row[2]))}</td><td>{money(Number(row[3]))}</td><td>{row[4]}</td></tr>)}</tbody><tfoot><tr><th scope="row">Total</th>{totals.map(total => <td><strong>{money(total)}</strong></td>)}<td>Three months: {money(totals.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0))}</td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
      <h3>Explain the increase</h3><p>The bill rises from {money(totals[0])} to {money(totals[2])} per month, an increase of {money(totals[2]-totals[0])}. Compute and AI explain $7,000 of that $9,000 change. Growth alone does not tell us whether the spending is wasteful or worthwhile.</p>
      <h3>Check the cost of the result</h3><p>Assume the matched AI records show 10,000 accepted results in Month 1 and 20,000 in Month 3. Direct provider cost per accepted result rises from $2,000 ÷ 10,000 = $0.20 to $5,000 ÷ 20,000 = $0.25, a 25% increase. Accepted volume doubles while the provider bill grows by 150%.</p><p>This is a provider-cost measure, not the full cost of a usable result. Human checking, retries, infrastructure allocation, and comparable quality need their own records. A change in the mix of tasks may explain the increase.</p>
      <h3>Leave with a decision plan</h3><ol><li>Reconcile billed charges with usage records and identify costs without an owner.</li><li>Ask the product and engineering owners which usage, model, retention, or configuration changes explain the increase.</li><li>Compare matched task types and quality before recommending a cheaper AI route.</li><li>Build a growth scenario using explicit volume and cost assumptions; keep it separate from a forecast.</li><li>Prioritize changes by financial impact, implementation effort, and risk. Confirm the next bill against the agreed actions.</li></ol><p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> explain the growth, investigate the rising AI unit cost, and give each next check an owner. The records do not yet support a savings estimate or a recommendation to change architecture.</p><p class="scope-note">The review shows the starting costs, what drove the change, which records are missing, and what to do next. Complex allocation, commitment analysis, implementation, and ongoing monitoring are separately agreed.</p>
      <p>Want to try a free tool first? <a href="/insights/using-cloud-cost-guard">Review your own cloud bill</a> or <a href="/insights/using-ai-cost-lens">compare AI plans</a>. Each walkthrough explains what to bring and how to use the result.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="ai-bill" class="example-section" aria-labelledby="ai-bill-title">
      <p class="offer-eyebrow">Technology Cost Review · AI spending</p>
      <h2 id="ai-bill-title">“Why doesn't the usage report<br />match the AI bill?”</h2>
      <p>In this fictional example, a team supplies a $1,200 invoice and usage records accounting for $1,080. The records cover the same billing period and currency. The unexplained difference is $120.</p>
      <h3>Work out what the records explain</h3>
      <p>Check which accounts, projects, charge types, adjustments and dates each export includes. Match the supported charges to the invoice and list the records still needed. The $120 is an amount to investigate, not an established error, wasted spending or money the team can recover.</p>
      <h3>Leave with a clear next check</h3>
      <p>You’d receive a reconciliation showing the matched $1,080, the unexplained $120 and the records needed to explain the difference. A bill alone cannot show which outputs were useful or support a cost per usable result conclusion.</p>
      <p class="scope-note">This is a fictional scope illustration, not a client result or a completed product test. Investigation and follow-up are agreed around the records available. Implementation and ongoing monitoring are separate scopes.</p>
      <p>Comparing two plans yourself? <a href="/insights/using-ai-cost-lens">Start with the free AI Cost Lens walkthrough</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="workflow" class="example-section" aria-labelledby="workflow-title"><p class="offer-eyebrow">Workflow Review</p><h2 id="workflow-title">“Can AI stop us copying the same numbers<br />into every report?”</h2><p>A fictional consultancy produces eight monthly project reports using a time tracker, accounting exports, and a spreadsheet. All timings below are assumed.</p><h3>Follow the work</h3><p>Export time and invoices → align project names → copy rows → calculate labor and expenses → check totals and missing entries → prepare a summary.</p><p>The first things to check are whether project identifiers match across the exports and whether the same rows keep being copied. AI-written commentary would not fix those inputs and could add checking work.</p>
      <h3>Compare options</h3><ul><li><strong>Controlled reporting template:</strong> first pilot; standardize identifiers and calculations.</li><li><strong>Existing native features:</strong> check actual plans and export capabilities before buying anything.</li><li><strong>Small automation:</strong> consider after inputs are consistent, with an owner for failures and maintenance.</li><li><strong>AI commentary:</strong> defer until input sensitivity, accuracy checks, and human judgment are addressed.</li></ul>
      <h3>One pilot, with checks</h3><p>Use two completed projects and redacted exports. Reconcile supplied totals, flag missing hours, flag duplicates, keep unmatched project identifiers visible, and confirm that an added expense updates its report. A named staff member owns updates and final review.</p>
      <h3>Capacity is different from cash saving</h3><p>Eight reports × 90 minutes = 12 hours/month. A target of eight × 45 minutes = six hours, plus one hour of maintenance, would free five net hours/month. At an assumed $40/hour, that is $200/month of capacity value.</p><p><strong>That is not $200 of cash saving.</strong> A financial saving requires an actual expense reduction; revenue benefit needs evidence of productive use. The target has not been measured.</p><p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> get the records and calculations right before adding AI. Compare actual preparation, checking, maintenance, exceptions, and usability across two report cycles. Implementation is separately scoped.</p>
    </section><div class="offer-actions"><a class="offer-button" href="/services#inquiry">Tell me about your question →</a></div>
  </div></main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .table-scroll table { min-width: 52rem; }
  .table-scroll caption { padding: .6rem .85rem; text-align: left; font-size: .85rem; }
  .table-hint { display: none; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .table-hint { display: block; margin-bottom: .5rem; font-size: .9rem; color: var(--theme-muted, #505a54); } }
</style>
